# A tOS machine's ~/.bashrc, installed as /root/.bashrc and /etc/skel/.bashrc.
#
# This file exists because of how bash reads its configuration, which is not
# how ash and dash read theirs. A pane runs an interactive shell that is *not*
# a login shell, and for that case bash reads ~/.bashrc and nothing else: not
# /etc/profile, and not $ENV — bash consults ENV only when it is invoked as
# `sh`. The live image used ENV to get the banner into every pane, because ash
# and dash do read it for every interactive shell, and that mechanism is the
# thing #82 is about replacing rather than the shell itself.

# A shell with no terminal has nothing to configure and must print nothing:
# scp and rsync open one and take whatever it says as protocol.
case $- in
    *i*) ;;
    *) return ;;
esac

# Enough history to be worth having, and none of the entries nobody wants: a
# duplicate of the line above, or a line deliberately hidden with a space.
HISTSIZE=10000
HISTFILESIZE=20000
HISTCONTROL=ignoreboth
shopt -s histappend
# A pane is resized by dragging a divider, which is a SIGWINCH the shell has
# to act on or every line it draws afterwards wraps in the wrong column.
shopt -s checkwinsize

# The compositor advertises xterm-256color and draws true colour, so a prompt
# with colour in it is safe here in a way it would not be on a terminal that
# has to be guessed at. Colours are set with \[ \] around them so bash knows
# they take no width; without that, recalling a long line wraps wrongly.
#
# The name is the session's own green, #92f980 — `Chrome::ACCENT`, the green of
# the tOS in the picture a machine opens with, and what every highlight tOS
# draws is in since #136. Written as a true colour rather than as an index into
# the 256, because the palette is what applications ask for by name and is not
# tOS's to theme (`docs/design/splash.md`); this file is tOS's own, shipped as
# /root/.bashrc and /etc/skel/.bashrc, so the colour in it is tOS choosing one
# for something tOS ships. It used to be 38;5;110 — #87afd7 — which left the
# line a person reads all day as the last blue on the machine (#141).
#
# The path keeps its own colour. One themed thing and one neutral one is what
# the rest of the chrome does: the accent marks what the session is pointing
# at, and a prompt in two accents points at nothing.
PS1='\[\e[38;2;146;249;128m\]\u@\h\[\e[0m\]:\[\e[38;5;180m\]\w\[\e[0m\]\$ '

alias ls='ls --color=auto'
alias grep='grep --color=auto'
alias ip='ip -color=auto'

# The message of the day and the install hint. A login shell reaches this
# through /etc/profile, which sources /etc/profile.d; an interactive pane has
# no other way to it, and the file itself only speaks once per session.
[ -r /etc/profile.d/tos.sh ] && . /etc/profile.d/tos.sh
